window.docContents[37].resolve({"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs":{"contents":"* Subgraph.mycielskianLeft: lift of a subgraph in G to a subgraph in mμ[G].* matchingNum / ν G: matching number of G (in ℕ∞, mirroring\nSimpleGraph.vertexCoverNum), the maximum number of edges in a matching of G.* Subgraph.boxProdLeft: a subgraph M of G copied into every G-fibre of\nG □ H.* hypercube.matching: perfect matching of Q[n + 1], defined recursively as ⊤ on\nQ[1] and the fibre-copy of the previous matching thereafter.* Subgraph.mycielskianDouble: the subgraph of mμ[G] with edges x ₗ — y ᵣ and\nx ᵣ — y ₗ for each edge x — y of a subgraph of G.* Subgraph.mycielskianDoubleDartEmbedding: embedding of the darts of a subgraph\ninto the edge set of its Mycielskian double.* ColoringOn: colouring of every vertex of G that need only be proper on the\nedges of G with both ends in s.* ColorableOn: predicate for the existence of a G.ColoringOn (Fin n) s.* ColoringOn.restrict: restriction of a G.ColoringOn γ s to any t ⊆ s.* ColoringOn.copy: transport a G.ColoringOn γ s along a proof that s = t.* ColoringOn.toColoring: a G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ.* Coloring.toColoringOn: the ColoringOn of a set s induced by a colouring of\nthe whole graph.* ColoringOn.empty: the colouring of the empty set of vertices by a given colour\nc.* ColoringOn.singleton: colouring of {a} by any colour c.* ColoringOn.ofNotAdj: colouring of {a, b} by a single colour c when\n¬ G.Adj a b.* ColoringOn.union: combination of compatible colourings of s and t into a\ncolouring of s ∪ t.* ColoringOn.insert: extension of a colouring of s to insert a s using a colour\nnot used on neighbours of a in s.* ColoringOn.map: relabelling of the colours of a partial colouring along an\ninjective map.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"Chapter 9: Subgraphs","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-9-subgraphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-computations":{"contents":"As well as reasoning about graphs in Lean we can also do computations with them. For\nexample we can check that 0 and 3 are adjacent in the complete graph on Fin 5\n(the type consisting of {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}).\n\n#eval (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 5)).Adj 0 3   -- true\n\n\nWe can count edges in finite graphs. For example the completeGraph on Fin 6 has\n15 edges.\n\n#eval (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 6)).edgeFinset.card -- 15\n\n\nEven when our goal is to prove theorems, computations such as these can be very\nuseful. Firstly, they allow us to perform quick sanity checks of our definitions\nand secondly they can be used inside proofs with the decide tactic.\n\nexample : (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 6)).Adj 2 3 := by\n  decide\n\n\nBoth #eval and decide rely on adjacency being decidable: Lean needs a concrete\nmethod for deciding whether or not any given pair of vertices are adjacent. We will\nreturn to this, and to computations with graphs more generally, in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.8. Computations","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-computations"}});